29976: 2. **"Install no third-party dependency" vs "the full suite".** The upgrade itself (three tools + two new modules) is proven stdlib-only by a dedicated fail-closed step, and those modules run **before any install**. But the *pre-existing* suite needs the project's own declared `jsonschema>=4.0`, which cannot be present on a bare runner — so the workflow has exactly one install step, resolving the project's own metadata (`pip install -e .`) with a comment explaining there is no upgrade requirements file and none is pulled. `requirements.lock` is deliberately not used as an install recipe. This is the one place where a literal reading of the brief and a runnable full suite conflict; I kept the suite runnable and documented it.
29977: 3. **No runner available here**, so the workflow itself was not executed; I validated the YAML parse and executed each step's script text locally (bash 5.2 / CPython 3.11.17) where the step did not require GitHub-only context.
29978: 4. **Concurrent lanes.** `tools/build_public_plugin.py`, `tools/build_web_fallback.py`, `packaging/public-plugin/*` and the two new test modules were being written by sibling lanes (B1/B2/B4) during this run, so their contents may still move; my four files do not depend on their internals beyond the pinned tool CLIs, refusal codes and test-module names, which I verified against the working tree.

